      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2017 The Authors. Published by Elsevier Ltd.. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "INTRODUCTION",
            "content": "Primary pulmonary meningiomas (PPMs) are very rare neoplasms, and we reported the first multiple case of PPMs in 1998. Usually, PPMs are slow-growing tumors with a good prognosis. Herein, we reported a rare multiple case of PPMs with an extremely long clinical course."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
            "Label": "PRESENTATION OF THE CASE",
            "content": "An 84-year-old Japanese woman with a history of multiple PPMs histologically confirmed as having a right-sided slowly growing lung nodule for 20 years. In 2010, we also reported the additional clinical course in the initial case 10 years after surgery implying a benign biological nature. Subsequent to that report, new lesions appeared in the bilateral lung fields with quite a slow growth rate. She was asymptomatic, and no tumor was observed in the cranial cavity during the course of her disease."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "DISCUSSION AND CONCLUSION",
            "content": "This case illustrates rare multiple PPMs and highlights their biological behavior with very slow progression from a most likely benign tumor over a 20-year period."
          }
        ]
      },
